.docname {Domain in code}
.include {docs}

The [Domain](domain.qd) page never mentions code. This page is the bridge: for each part of the
domain, the type that holds it, the step that makes it, and the file the twin is written to.

- Types live in `backend/include/vcortex/domain/`.
- The twin file is described by `backend/schema/twin.schema.json`.

## From the domain to the code

| Domain | C++ type | File | Made by |
|---|---|---|---|
| Survey (one walk) | `Snapshot` plus a `Trajectory` | `snapshot.hpp` | `io/store`, filled by what the robot sends |
| Map | `Snapshot::points`, a list of `LidarPoint` | `snapshot.hpp`, `lidar_point.hpp` | `io/store` |
| Path | `Trajectory`, a list of `Pose` | `snapshot.hpp` | `io/store` (keeps one pose every 0.2 m) |
| Building | `Building` | `building.hpp` | `model/model` |
| Level | `Level` | `building.hpp` | `model/model` |
| Wall | `Wall` | `building.hpp` | `model/wall/walls` |
| Room | `Room` | `building.hpp` | `model/rooms` |
| Object | `Object` | `building.hpp` | `model/object/objects` |
| Wall height, and where it came from | `Level::wall_height` and `HeightSource` (`Default` or `User`) | `building.hpp`, `settings.hpp` | `model/model`, from the settings |

## What the types keep

| Type | Fields | Note |
|---|---|---|
| `LidarPoint` | position, intensity, `num_scan_seen` | `num_scan_seen` is how many scans saw this spot: the "seen in many scans is solid" rule |
| `Pose` | time, position, `yaw` | Where the robot was, and which way it faced |
| `Level` | `floor_z`, `wall_height`, `wall_height_source`, walls, rooms, objects | One level per building today |
| `Wall` | `start`, `end`, `thickness`, `num_points` | A straight piece, seen from above |
| `Room` | `outline`, `wall_ids`, `closed`, optional `name` | `closed` is false when the outline has a gap |
| `Object` | `center`, `size`, `yaw`, `bottom_z`, `top_z_seen`, optional `room_id`, `kind` | `top_z_seen` says "seen", not "real top" |

The names `Wall`, `Room` and `Object` follow IFC, the open standard for building data:

| Ours | IFC name |
|---|---|
| `Wall` | `IfcWall` |
| `Room` | `IfcSpace` |
| `Object` | `IfcBuildingElementProxy` (a "thing" with no better class) |

## Domain rules in the code

| Rule | Where you see it |
|---|---|
| A wall height belongs to the level | It is a field of `Level`, not of `Wall` |
| A twin says where the wall height came from | `wall_height_source` is always set |
| A room may be open | `Room::closed` |
| An object belongs to at most one room | `Object::room_id` is optional; empty means the level |
| What was not seen stays unknown | `top_z_seen`, not `top_z` |
| Furniture is not a wall | `find_objects` only receives the dots that are left after the walls and the floor |

## Ids and references

Parts point at each other by id, as text.

| Part | Id | Points at |
|---|---|---|
| Level | `level-1` | nothing |
| Wall | `wall-1`, `wall-2`, … | nothing |
| Room | `room-1`, `room-2`, … | its walls, in `wall_ids` |
| Object | `object-1`, `object-2`, … | its room, in `room_id` (or `null`) |

- Ids are numbered in the order the step made them. Walls are numbered after sorting.
- Ids are **only good inside one twin**. Run the model again and `wall-1` may be another wall.
- The JSON writer refuses a twin where an id is empty or repeated, where a `wall_ids` or
  `room_id` points at nothing, or where a room outline has fewer than 3 points. A reader can
  follow every link without checking it.

## Units and frames

| What | Value |
|---|---|
| Length | metres |
| Up | the z axis |
| Plan (walls, rooms, objects) | x and y only, seen from above |
| Height | `floor_z` of the level, plus `bottom_z` and `top_z_seen` on objects |
| Frame | the map frame the robot named (`camera_init` in the example below) |
| Rounding | millimetres, in the JSON and the DXF |
| Angles | **radians** in an object's `yaw`, **degrees** in the report (`yaw_deg`, `floor_tilt_deg`) |

- The building comes back in the robot's own map frame, **not turned square**. So it lines up
  with the raw cloud with no extra step.
- `floor_z` is the floor height under the middle of the walls. The floor may be tilted, so the
  height at the map's origin could lie metres away from the building.
- A wall is drawn by its **centre line**; `thickness` is added on both sides.

## The twin file

### A real example

One real run: the room from the [Overview](introduction.qd), 192,149 dots, offline mode. The
file is cut to one wall, the room and one object (the real file has 4 walls and 19 objects).

```json
{
  "schema_version": "0.2.0",
  "run_id": 0,
  "seq": 0,
  "frame": { "id": "camera_init", "up": "z", "unit": "m" },
  "report": {
    "points_in": 192149,
    "after_outliers": 186427,
    "floor_tilt_deg": 2.3575199762923114,
    "free_space_area_m2": 24.025000000000002,
    "free_space_rooms": 1,
    "outline_sides": 4,
    "yaw_deg": 16.8,
    "duration_ms": 1570.293228
  },
  "levels": [
    {
      "id": "level-1",
      "floor_z": -0.455,
      "wall_height": 2.7,
      "wall_height_source": "default",
      "walls": [
        { "id": "wall-1", "start": [-5.402, -5.208], "end": [-7.294, 1.058],
          "thickness": 0.1, "points": 761 }
      ],
      "rooms": [
        { "id": "room-1", "name": null, "closed": true,
          "outline": [[-5.35, -5.115], [2.644, -2.701], [0.794, 3.426], [-7.2, 1.012]],
          "wall_ids": ["wall-1", "wall-2", "wall-3", "wall-4"] }
      ],
      "objects": [
        { "id": "object-1", "kind": "unknown", "room_id": "room-1",
          "center": [-5.469, -2.565], "size": [0.5, 2.7], "yaw": 0.2932153143350474,
          "bottom_z": -0.305, "top_z_seen": 1.217, "points": 5292 }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

Read it like this:

- `wall-1` runs from (-5.402, -5.208) to (-7.294, 1.058): length
  √(1.892² + 6.266²) = **6.55 m**. This matches the room's short side.
- `room-1` is closed and lists all 4 walls.
- `object-1` stands in `room-1`. Its box is 0.5 × 2.7 m, turned by 0.293 rad (16.8°, the same
  turn the model used to square the map). The highest dot seen is 1.217 m above the map's zero, so
  **1.217 − (−0.455) = 1.67 m** above the floor.

### The top level

| Field | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `schema_version` | Version of the file's shape, as `major.minor.patch` |
| `run_id`, `seq` | Which survey, and how many map pieces it had when the model ran |
| `frame` | Name of the map frame, `up` is always `"z"`, `unit` is always `"m"` |
| `report` | What the model found, to judge a map without opening it |
| `levels` | A list with at least one level |

### The report

| Field | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `points_in` | Dots in the map |
| `after_outliers` | Dots left after dropping stray ones |
| `floor_tilt_deg` | Slope of the floor. A few degrees means the sensor was tilted |
| `free_space_area_m2` | Floor the robot could reach, counted from its path, also under desks |
| `free_space_rooms` | Rooms the robot's path walked into |
| `outline_sides` | Wall sides found, all rooms together. Fewer than 4 per room means a side had no wall face |
| `yaw_deg` | The turn that squared the map |
| `duration_ms` | Time of the run |

## The schema is the contract

- `twin.schema.json` says what a twin may hold. **No other field is allowed**
  (`additionalProperties` is false everywhere), so a typo cannot slip in.
- The version follows one rule: **major** when a field is renamed or removed, **minor** when an
  optional field is added.
- The frontend does not copy the shape by hand. `npm run generate-types` builds
  `frontend/src/types/twin.ts` from the schema.
- `kind` can only be `"unknown"` today. See [Domain](domain.qd) for what is missing.

## Gaps found

| What | Where |
|---|---|
| The domain says an object outside every room belongs to the level. The code gives an object a `room_id` only when the room is **closed**; an object in an open room gets `null`, as if it were in no room | `model/model.cpp` |
| The domain says heights start from the floor. An object's `bottom_z` and `top_z_seen` are the raw z of its lowest and highest dot in the map, not the height above the floor at that spot. On a tilted floor the two differ (see [Objects](objects.qd)) | `model/object/objects.cpp` |
| A room's outline is the box around the free floor, so an L-shaped room gets an outline that is too big (a TODO in the code) | `model/rooms.cpp` |
| The HTTP types `Status`, `Settings` and `ModelReport` of the frontend are written by hand, not built from a schema. The status report calls the floor slope `tilt_deg`; the twin file calls it `floor_tilt_deg` | `frontend/src/services/api.ts` |
| The twin's `report` has no `poses`, `walls`, `rooms` or `objects` counts; only the status answer has them | `schema/twin.schema.json`, `io/server.cpp` |
